Let us call a point in the $xy$-plane a good point if each of its coordinates is an integer from $1$ to $2000$. A polyline $ABCD$ is called a Z-shaped polyline if it satisfies:
* $x_1 < x_2$, $y_1 = y_2$;
* $x_2 < x_3$, $y_2 - x_2 = y_3 - x_3$;
* $x_3 < x_4$, $y_3 = y_4$.
Determine the smallest possible positive integer $n$ such that $n$ Z-shaped polylines cover all good points.

Let us call a point in the $xy$-plane a good point if each of its coordinates is an integer from $1$ to $2000$. A polyline $ABCD$ is called a Z-shaped polyline if it satisfies:
* $x_1 < x_2$, $y_1 = y_2$;
* $x_2 > x_3$, $y_2 + x_2 = y_3 + x_3$;
* $x_3 < x_4$, $y_3 = y_4$.
Determine the smallest possible positive integer $n$ such that $n$ Z-shaped polylines cover all good points.

Let us call a point in the $xy$-plane a good point if each of its coordinates is an integer from $1$ to $2000$. A polyline $ABCD$ is called a Z-shaped polyline if it satisfies:
* $x_1 < x_2$, $y_1 = y_2$;
* $x_2 > x_3$, $y_2 - x_2 = y_3 - x_3$;
* $x_3 < x_4$, $y_3 = y_4 + 1$.
Determine the smallest possible positive integer $n$ such that $n$ Z-shaped polylines cover all good points.

positive_text:
Three strictly increasing sequences of positive integers $A = (A_1, A_2, A_3, \ldots)$, $B = (B_1, B_2, B_3, \ldots)$, and $C = (C_1, C_2, C_3, \ldots)$ are defined such that each positive integer is in exactly one of the sequences. For all $n \geq 1$:
(i) $C_{A_n} = B_n + 1$;
(ii) $A_{n+1} > B_n$;
(iii) the value $C_n C_{n+1} - n C_{n+1} - (n+1) C_n$ is even.
Determine $A_{2010}, B_{2010}$, and $C_{2010}$.

negatives.text:
Three strictly increasing sequences of positive integers $a_n$, $b_n$, and $c_n$ partition the positive integers. For every $n$:
(i) $c_{a_n} = b_n - 1$;
(ii) $a_{n+1} > b_n$;
(iii) $c_n c_{n+1} - (n+1) c_{n+1} - n c_n$ is even.
Find $a_{2010}, b_{2010}, c_{2010}$.

