positive_text:
Let $\mathbb{Z}[x]$ be the ring of single-variable polynomials with integer coefficients. Determine all functions $\theta : \mathbb{Z}[x] \to \mathbb{Z}$ satisfying for all $p, q \in \mathbb{Z}[x]$:\begin{itemize}
    \item $\theta(p + 1) = \theta(p) + 1$, and
    \item if $\theta(p) \neq 0$, then $\theta(p)$ divides $\theta(pq)$.
\end{itemize}

negatives.text:
Let $\mathbb{Z}[x]$ be the set of all polynomials with integer coefficients in variable $x$. Find all functions $\theta : \mathbb{Z}[x] \to \mathbb{Z}$ such that for any $p, q \in \mathbb{Z}[x]$:\begin{itemize}
    \item $\theta(p + 1) = \theta(p) + 1$, and
    \item if $\theta(p) \neq 0$, then $\theta(pq) < \theta(p)$.
\end{itemize}

Let $\mathbb{Z}[x]$ be the set of all polynomials with integer coefficients in variable $x$. Find all functions $\theta : \mathbb{Z}[x] \to \mathbb{Z}$ such that for any $p, q \in \mathbb{Z}[x]$:\begin{itemize}
    \item $\theta(p + 1) = \theta(p) + 1$, and
    \item if $\theta(p) \neq 0$, then $\theta(p)$ divides $\theta(p + q)$.
\end{itemize}

Let $\mathbb{Z}[x]$ be the set of all polynomials with integer coefficients in variable $x$. Find all functions $\theta : \mathbb{Z}[x] \to \mathbb{Z}$ such that for any $p, q \in \mathbb{Z}[x]$:\begin{itemize}
    \item $\theta(p + 1) = \theta(p) - 1$, and
    \item if $\theta(p) \neq 0$, then $\theta(p)$ divides $\theta(p \cdot q)$.
\end{itemize}

positive_text:
Let $n > 1$ be a positive integer. Consider an $n \times n$ grid of unit squares, each colored black or white, satisfying the following properties:
- The black squares form a connected region (via shared edges);
- The white squares also form a connected region;
- Every $2 \times 2$ block in the grid contains both black and white squares.

Find, with justification, the largest possible absolute difference between the number of black and white squares in the grid, as a function of $n$.

negatives.text:
Let $n > 1$ be a positive integer. Each unit square in an $n \times n$ grid of squares is colored either black or white, such that the following conditions hold:
- Any two black squares can be connected by a sequence of black squares where every two consecutive squares in the sequence share an edge;
- Any two white squares can be connected by a sequence of white squares where every two consecutive squares in the sequence share an edge;
- Any $2 \times 2$ subgrid contains at least one black square.

Determine the maximum possible difference between the number of black squares and white squares in this grid (in terms of $n$).

Let $n > 1$ be a positive integer. Each unit square in an $n \times n$ grid of squares is colored either black or white, such that the following conditions hold:
- Any two black squares can be connected by a sequence of black squares where every two consecutive squares in the sequence share an edge;
- Any two white squares can be connected by a sequence of white squares where every two consecutive squares in the sequence share an edge;
- Any $2 \times 2$ subgrid contains at most one square of each color.

Determine the maximum possible difference between the number of black squares and white squares in this grid (in terms of $n$).

Let $n > 1$ be a positive integer. Each unit square in an $n \times n$ grid of squares is colored either black or white, such that the following conditions hold:
- Any two black squares can be connected by a sequence of black squares where every two consecutive squares in the sequence share an edge;
- Any two white squares can be connected by a sequence of white squares where every two consecutive squares in the sequence share an edge;
- Any $2 \times 2$ subgrid contains at least one square of each color, or all squares are the same color.

Determine the maximum possible difference between the number of black squares and white squares in this grid (in terms of $n$).

